I. Objective
You are an expert in PFAS adsorption studies. Your task is to enumerate ALL unique Performance_id (see definition below) values present in the provided source chunk(s). Do not omit any eligible value. Do not use abbreviated placeholders such as “and so on”. Each unique Performance_id must be listed explicitly.

Your enumeration is based on:
(1) Source chunk(s) from a paper. Scan all chunks with equal priority. Do not overlook eligible Performance_id values described in prose sentences. 
(2) Adsorbent_list is a controlled vocabulary generated by an earlier study-level adsorbent enumeration process. It aims to list all adsorbents used in the current study and is organized into In-scope adsorbents and Out-of-scope adsorbents. Each entry may include Adsorbent_id, full name, abbreviation, commercial name, category, and subcategory when that information is extractable. Use In-scope adsorbents to standardize adsorbent identities only when the current source text supports the match. Use Out-of-scope adsorbents as an exclusion list: if a source adsorbent matches an out-of-scope entry, do not enumerate a Performance_id for it. The provided Adsorbent_list is used for label standardization and scope disambiguation only; it is not evidence by itself.
(3) Water_type_list, a controlled vocabulary used only to standardize Water_type labels when water matrix is the Differentiating_Condition and matches entries in the list. Each entry include the full name, abbreviation, and water type for a single water matrix, if a field is not shown, it means it was not extracted. The provided Water_type_list is a controlled vocabulary for standardizing water labels; it is not evidence by itself. 

II. Scope of Enumeration
Don't assume every chunk has extractable Performance_id. Evaluate eligibility independently for each candidate Performance_id. A candidate Performance_id is in scope only if it satisfies all conditions below:

1. It reports **absolute numeric values** of **target performance matrices** for PFAS batch adsorption test, during which PFAS-containing liquid is contacted with a freshly prepared adsorbent in a closed-vessel to measure PFAS uptake/removal by adsorption under defined conditions **without** energy input. Regeneration, desorption, reuse-cycle batch tests are out of scope because those tests evaluate the performance of a spent, regenerated, or reused adsorbent rather than the initial adsorption performance of a freshly prepared adsorbent. Similarily, adsorption with energy input such as electricity, ultrasonic irradiation is also out-of-scope and excluded from output. **Target performance metrices** include Freundlich isotherm parameters Kf and 1/n (or n) (Qe=KF.Ce^(1/n)), Langmuir isotherm parameters Qm and KL (Qe=Qm.KL.Ce/(1+KL.Ce)), linear isotherm parameters Kd (Kd=Qe/Ce), equilibrium solid-phase uptake Qe (Qe=(C0-Ce).V/m), removal rate R (R=(C0-Ct)/C0*100%), pseudo-first-order (PFO) k1 and Qe (dQ/dt = k1 (Qe - Q)), and pseudo-second-order (PSO) k2 and Qe (dQ/dt = k2 (Qe-Q)^2)). Do not identify these metrics solely by their symbols. Instead, use their physical meaning and contextual role in the study. For example, Qe may be described as the solid-phase concentration, sorbed concentration, adsorbed amount, equilibrium uptake, or time-specific uptake in some studies and some people use Cs as the symbol.

2. The performance matrices should be on target adsorbents, the provided Adsorbent_list can be treated as the authoritative source for determining whether an adsorbent is in scope or out of scope in most cases. 

3. Performance matrices must describe PFAS adsorption performance. Performance matrices for non-PFAS compounds should be excluded, even if PFAS are present in the experiment. For example, “47% of diethylene glycol monobutyl ether (DGBE) was removed in the presence of 100 ng/L PFOS” should not be included because the reported performance refers to DGBE, not PFOS.

4. Base decisions only on evidence provided in the source chunks. Performance_id must be based on contents original to the current study and directly reported in the current source text under consideration. Do not enumerate previous work (e.g., "as reported by Smith et al.") or pointers to missing content (e.g., "see Table S1" unless the actual data is also present in the current source text).


